  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Similar weather pattern expected today compared\nto yesterday, but this time with slightly better moisture as\nPWATs climb back to near 2\". However, mid-level subsidence will\nbe increasing as heights rise ahead of approaching ridge. This\nwill likely quash more widespread activity, but with enough\ninstability and sufficient lift from sea and sound breezes the\nmore routine convection may briefly make an appearance this\nafternoon (Cedar Island and Stumpy Point cells come to mind).\nAny activity that does develop will quickly fall off around\nsunset.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unusually strong mid-level ridge centered over \nthe central MS River Valley this morning will continue to \ngradually build towards the Carolinas and mid-Atlantic into mid \nto late week. Underneath the risk, expansive area of anomalously\nwarm low-level thicknesses will be in place across much of the \nsoutheastern CONUS as 850mb temperatures soar to between 20-25C,\ntranslating to surface highs well into the 90s to potentially \n100 degrees. Additionally, persistent southerly surface flow as \nhigh pressure sits off the coast will keep dew points elevated \nin the 70s most days and evenings, especially from Wednesday \ninto Friday.\n\nThe combination of the heat and oppressive moisture will make it\nfeel like 105-110 degrees across a large portion of eastern NC\nmid to late week. ECMWF's EFI continues to show a strong signal\nfor anomalous highs for the period and even stronger for warm\novernight lows, boosting confidence to this heat wave's impact.\nThe NWS experimental Heat Risk puts a large portion of ENC in\nthe \"Extreme\" impact category both Thursday and Friday but\n\"Major\" impacts as early as tomorrow.\n\nHeat headlines will likely be needed starting Wednesday and run\ninto late week. Heat headlines are very rare for our area in\nSeptember - since 2005, the only time they have been issued for\nthe month by our office was in 2020.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A strong cold front is forecast to move through\nthe Carolinas on Saturday into Sunday. At minimum, this will \nknock down the heat, bringing a refreshing break from the \nanomalous heat and humidity. Additionally, guidance show a \ndecent signal for convection with the front. Synoptically, the \nsetup appears favorable for strong to severe thunderstorms \ndepending on the timing of the passage of the front. Machine \nlearning guidance shows a decent signal for this potential as \nwell. From a probabilistic standpoint, the risk appears to be \nthe highest on Saturday.\n\nBehind the front, highs are forecast to drop back down into the\n80s, with lows in the 60s. Adding to this will be dewpoints \nfalling back into the 60s making it feel more comfortable.",
